## Overview
We aim to assess the performance of various models on the prediction of pollutant concentrations, and apply these methods to obtain a predicted map in a certain region or at certain locations. I focused on log-transformed, unweighted annual average concentrations of ultrafine particles (UFP) and black carbon (BC).

## Method
The methods being considered were 

* universal kriging with exponential covariance combined with partial least squares (UK-PLS)
* spatial random forest based on cross-validation (SpatRF-CV)
* spatial random forest based on pseudo likelihood (SpatRF-PL)
* random forest for independent data (RF)
* smoothing via thin plate regression spline (TPRS)
* RF followed by TPRS on the residuals (RF-TPRS)
* TPRS followed by RF on the residuals (TPRS-RF)

UK-PLS was a two-step procedure where PLS components of the design matrix were first extracted, and in the next step universal kriging with exponential covariance was run with the top PLS components. The number of components to use was selected via 5-fold cross-validation (CV).

RF and TPRS were run with built-in R packages ```randomForest``` and ```mgcv```. RF-TPRS and TPRS-RF were two-step procedures where one model (RF or TPRS) was run in the first step, and the residuals were fitted by the the other (TPRS or RF) model.

For SpatRF, both the pseudo likelihood and the nonparametric approach were investigated. In addition to the covariance parameter that was found via grid search (by optimizing the pseudo likelihood for SpatRF-PL or by cross-validation for SpatRF-CV), three parameters were tuned by random search (to narrow down the range), followed by grid search within a smaller range:

* Number of covariates to sample for each tree
* Minimum leaf size (and in turn, the maximum number of leaves to use)
* Number of observations to use in each tree

These four parameters were selected based on out-of-sample errors of the spatial random forest.

## Assessing the performance via cross-validation
First, 5-fold CV was conducted to compare the performance of each model, with their accuracy summarized below by $R^2$.

```{r, echo = FALSE}
combn_ests = function(rslt, rslt2 = NULL, rslt3 = NULL, rslt4 = NULL, varname){
  # combine estimates
  time.pls <- time.spatrf <- 0
  Y.pls.TPRS = Y.pca.spatrf.cv = Y.pca.spatrf.pl = rep(NA, nrow(X))
  for (j in 1:length(rslt)){
    idx <- !is.na(rslt[[j]]$Y.pls)
    Y.pls[idx] <- rslt[[j]]$Y.pls[idx]
    Y.spatrf.cv[idx] <- rslt[[j]]$Y.spatrf.cv[idx]
    Y.spatrf.pl[idx] <- rslt[[j]]$Y.spatrf.pl[idx]
    time.pls <- time.pls + rslt[[j]]$pls.time[3]
    time.spatrf <- time.spatrf + rslt[[j]]$spatrf.time[3]
    if (!is.null(rslt2)){
      Y.pls.TPRS[idx] <- rslt2[[j]]$Y.pls[idx]
      Y.pls.mean[idx] <- rslt2[[j]]$Y.pls.mean[idx]
    }
    if (!is.null(rslt3)){
      Y.tprs[idx] <- rslt3[[j]]$Y.tprs[idx]
      Y.tprs.rf[idx] <- rslt3[[j]]$Y.tprs.rf[idx]
      Y.rf[idx] <- rslt3[[j]]$Y.rf[idx]
      Y.rf.tprs[idx] <- rslt3[[j]]$Y.rf.tprs[idx]
    }
    if (!is.null(rslt4)){
      Y.pca.spatrf.cv[idx] <- rslt4[[j]]$Y.spatrf.cv[idx]
      Y.pca.spatrf.pl[idx] <- rslt4[[j]]$Y.spatrf.pl[idx]
    }
  }
  y = dat[,grepl(varname, colnames(dat))]
  R2.pls = 1 - mean((Y.pls - y)^2)/var(y)
  R2.pls.TPRS = ifelse(!is.null(rslt2), 1 - mean((Y.pls.TPRS - y)^2)/var(y), NA)
  R2.pls.mean = 1 - mean((Y.pls.mean - y)^2)/var(y)
  R2.spatrf.pl = 1 - mean((Y.spatrf.pl - y)^2)/var(y)
  R2.spatrf.cv = 1 - mean((Y.spatrf.cv - y)^2)/var(y)
  R2.tprs = ifelse(!is.null(rslt3), 1 - mean((Y.tprs - y)^2)/var(y), NA)
  R2.tprs.rf = 1 - mean((Y.tprs.rf - y)^2)/var(y)
  R2.rf = 1 - mean((Y.rf - y)^2)/var(y)
  R2.rf.tprs = 1 - mean((Y.rf.tprs - y)^2)/var(y)
  R2.pca.spatrf.pl = 1 - mean((Y.pca.spatrf.pl - y)^2)/var(y)
  R2.pca.spatrf.cv = 1 - mean((Y.pca.spatrf.cv - y)^2)/var(y)
  return(list(Y.pls = Y.pls, Y.pls.TPRS = Y.pls.TPRS, Y.pls.mean = Y.pls.mean,
              Y.spatrf.pl = Y.spatrf.pl, Y.spatrf.cv = Y.spatrf.cv,
              Y.tprs = Y.tprs, Y.tprs.rf = Y.tprs.rf, Y.rf = Y.rf, Y.rf.tprs = Y.rf.tprs,
              Y.pca.spatrf.pl = Y.pca.spatrf.pl, Y.pca.spatrf.cv = Y.pca.spatrf.cv,
              R2.pls = R2.pls, R2.pls.TPRS = R2.pls.TPRS, R2.pls.mean = R2.pls.mean,
              R2.spatrf.pl = R2.spatrf.pl, R2.spatrf.cv = R2.spatrf.cv,
              R2.tprs = R2.tprs, R2.tprs.rf = R2.tprs.rf, 
              R2.rf = R2.rf, R2.rf.tprs = R2.rf.tprs,
              R2.pca.spatrf.pl = R2.pca.spatrf.pl, R2.pca.spatrf.cv = R2.pca.spatrf.cv,
              time.pls = time.pls, time.spatrf = time.spatrf))
}

